RQ1 — Source Prominence: "Am I showing up, and where?"
Q1. Which engine gives sources the most visibility, measured by mean PAWC_norm across all 250 queries?

Visual: Horizontal bar chart, one bar per engine, sorted by mean PAWC_norm.

Why included: Shows which engine gives your content the most prominent placement.
Q2. Conditional on the engine actually searching, does the cross-engine PAWC_norm ranking change?

Visual: Paired bar chart, unconditional mean beside conditional mean per engine.

Why included: Separates engines that ignore your content from those that use it poorly.
Q3. How many sources does each engine cite per response, and does citing more sources raise or lower per-source prominence?

Visual: Scatter plot, x = mean sources cited, y = mean PAWC_norm, one point per engine.

Why included: Tells you whether more citations means each source gets less attention.
Q4. What proportion of queries produce a PAWC_norm of zero on each engine?

Visual: Stacked bar chart per engine, zero versus non-zero cells.

Why included: Shows how often each engine completely ignores external sources.
Q5. Does query intent (informational, navigational, transactional) predict source prominence, and does the effect differ by engine?

Visual: Grouped box plot, x = engine, colour = intent category, y = PAWC_norm.

Why included: Tells you which query types your content strategy should target per engine.

RQ2 — Reproducibility: "Can I trust this number?"
Q6. What is the median PAWC_norm CV and AIS CV per engine, and which engines produce stable readings?

Visual: Dot plot, two dots per engine (one for PAWC_norm CV, one for AIS CV).

Why included: Shows which engine's visibility score you can actually rely on.
Q7. What share of query-engine cells produce a CV above 0.50 on each engine?

Visual: Stacked bar chart per engine, cells below 0.50 versus at or above.

Why included: Tells you how often the measurement is too unstable to act on.
Q8. Is the instability driven by the engine intermittently not searching, or by genuine retrieval variation when it does search?

Visual: Faceted box plot, x = search-rate band (0–25%, 25–75%, 75–100%), y = PAWC_norm CV, faceted by engine.

Why included: Distinguishes engines that wobble from engines that sometimes just switch off.
Q9. Do all five engines agree on whether a given source is visible for a given query?

Visual: Heatmap, rows = queries (clustered), columns = engines, cell colour = PAWC_norm.

Why included: Shows whether optimising for one engine means anything on another.
Q10. How sensitive is the PAWC_norm engine ranking to the NLI entailment threshold, and does AIS remain stable across the full τ sweep?

Visual: Bump chart (line chart of engine rank by τ), one panel for PAWC_norm and one for AIS.

Why included: Tests whether the ranking holds or flips depending on a single setting.
Q11. At k = 8, what proportion of cells still sit on a mechanical CV ceiling, and what does this imply for the minimum viable run count?

Visual: Bar chart per engine, proportion of ceiling-hitting cells at k = 8 versus k = 3 (pilot comparison).

Why included: Tells practitioners the bare minimum number of checks before trusting a score.

RQ3 — Citation Accuracy: "When I show up, is the engine telling the truth?"
Q12. What is each engine's AIS rate?

Visual: Horizontal bar chart, one bar per engine, sorted by mean AIS.

Why included: Shows which engine most faithfully represents your content when citing it.
Q13. What proportion of sentence-source pairs fall in the indecisive NLI band (0.30–0.50)?

Visual: Stacked histogram of entailment scores, bands coloured (contradiction, indecisive, entailment), one panel per engine.

Why included: Reveals how many citations are borderline, not clearly right or wrong.
Q14. Do engines that cite more sources also cite more accurately, or is there a volume-accuracy trade-off?

Visual: Scatter plot, x = mean sources cited, y = mean AIS, one point per engine with error bars.

Why included: Tests whether engines that cite heavily are also citing honestly.
Q15. Are source prominence and citation accuracy correlated within each engine?

Visual: Faceted scatter plot, one panel per engine, x = PAWC_norm, y = AIS, with Spearman ρ annotated.

Why included: Shows whether high visibility also means accurate representation, or the opposite.
Q16. Does query intent affect citation accuracy, and do engines that perform well on informational queries also perform well on transactional queries?

Visual: Grouped box plot, x = engine, colour = intent category, y = AIS.

Why included: Tells you whether citation quality depends on what the user is asking about.

Cross-cutting: "Which engine should I care about, and how should I measure it?"
Q17. If the five engines are ranked separately on PAWC_norm, AIS, and CV, does any engine rank consistently at the top or bottom?

Visual: Radar chart, one polygon per engine, three axes (PAWC_norm, AIS, inverse CV).

Why included: Shows whether any single engine wins on everything, or every engine trades off.
Q18. How large is the gap between a single-run measurement and the k = 8 mean on each engine?

Visual: Error bar plot, x = engine, point = k = 8 mean, whiskers = range of individual run values.

Why included: Shows the cost of relying on one measurement instead of eight.
Q19. What is the non-claim segmentation artefact rate per engine, and does correcting for it change the cross-engine ranking?

Visual: Paired bar chart, raw versus cleaned PAWC_norm ranking per engine.

Why included: Tests whether engine output formatting tricks distort the visibility score.